/* data/detail-c/chem-12-11.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/chem-12-11.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-11"] = {"chem-12-11-b01":{"idea":"Alcohols are grouped by the carbon that carries the –OH group, called the carbinol carbon.","steps":["Find the carbon bonded to –OH.","Count how many other carbons are bonded to it.","One carbon neighbour: primary. Two: secondary. Three: tertiary."],"others":"The length of the chain does not decide the class. Hydrogens on the oxygen are always one in an alcohol. The number of –OH groups tells us mono-, di- or trihydric, which is a different grouping.","trap":"Count carbon neighbours of the –OH carbon, not the total –OH groups.","answer":"How many carbons are bonded to the carbon that carries the -OH"},"chem-12-11-b02":{"idea":"The prefix tells how many –OH groups a molecule has.","steps":["Di- means two.","Ethylene glycol, HOCH₂CH₂OH, has one –OH on each of its two carbons.","So it has 2 –OH groups."],"others":"One –OH is monohydric, like ethanol. Three –OH groups make a trihydric alcohol, like glycerol. Zero –OH groups would not be an alcohol at all.","trap":"Mono = 1, di = 2, tri = 3.","answer":"2"},"chem-12-11-b03":{"idea":"Water adds across a C=C bond in the presence of acid.","steps":["Propene + H₂O (H⁺ catalyst) is an addition reaction.","H goes to one carbon and –OH to the other.","By Markovnikov's rule, –OH goes to the more substituted carbon, giving propan-2-ol.","The product is an alcohol."],"others":"An alkane would need hydrogen added, not water. An ether needs two alkyl groups joined by oxygen. A carboxylic acid would need oxidation, which does not happen here.","answer":"An alcohol"},"chem-12-11-b04":{"idea":"Ethanol was made first by fermentation, and this is still a main route.","steps":["Yeast contains the enzyme zymase.","Zymase turns glucose into ethanol and CO₂.","C₆H₁₂O₆ → 2C₂H₅OH + 2CO₂.","This is called fermentation of sugars."],"others":"Reducing acetic acid with plain hydrogen and no catalyst does not work in practice. Electrolysis of pure water gives hydrogen and oxygen. Burning glucose gives CO₂ and water, not ethanol.","answer":"Fermentation of sugars using yeast (the enzyme zymase)"},"chem-12-11-b05":{"idea":"A phenol has –OH on the aromatic ring itself. Benzyl alcohol has it on a side-chain carbon.","steps":["In phenol, –OH is bonded directly to an sp² ring carbon.","In benzyl alcohol, C₆H₅–CH₂–OH, the –OH sits on the CH₂ outside the ring.","So the position of –OH decides the class and the behaviour."],"others":"The rings are the same in both: aromatic. Swapping the positions of –OH, as one choice does, is the reverse of the truth. Both compounds have one –OH, not two.","trap":"–OH on the ring means phenol. –OH on a side chain means alcohol.","answer":"In phenol, -OH sits directly on a ring carbon; in benzyl alcohol it is on the CH2 outside the ring"},"chem-12-11-b06":{"idea":"Hydrogen bonding raises boiling points.","steps":["The –OH group has a hydrogen on a very electronegative oxygen.","This hydrogen bonds to the oxygen of a neighbouring molecule.","These bonds are strong, so more heat is needed to separate the molecules.","Alkanes have only weak van der Waals forces."],"others":"Alcohols are not always heavier; we compare similar masses. Boiling point depends strongly on intermolecular forces. Alkanes of similar mass do not have more carbon atoms in any useful sense.","answer":"Alcohol molecules hydrogen-bond to each other via the -OH group"},"chem-12-11-b07":{"idea":"Ethers cannot hydrogen-bond with each other because they have no O–H.","steps":["An ether has C–O–C, with no hydrogen on oxygen.","So ether molecules do not hydrogen-bond to each other.","Their forces are weaker, so the boiling points are lower than for alcohols.","Ethers are also less reactive, since they have no –OH."],"others":"A larger mass from two alkyl groups does not beat hydrogen bonding at similar masses. The C–O–C bond is not strained. Ethers do not hydrogen-bond to each other, so similar boiling points is wrong.","trap":"Compare at similar molar mass.","answer":"Less reactive, with lower boiling points, as they cannot H-bond to each other"},"chem-12-11-b08":{"idea":"A Grignard reagent is an organometallic compound of magnesium.","steps":["It is made from R–X and Mg in dry ether.","Mg inserts between R and X.","So the formula is R–Mg–X.","R is bonded to Mg, and Mg to the halogen."],"others":"R–X–Mg is wrong because Mg bonds to carbon, not to the halogen end. R₂Mg has no halogen, and that is a different compound. R–MgOH would form from water, which destroys the reagent.","answer":"R-Mg-X: an alkyl/aryl group bonded to Mg, which is bonded to a halogen"},"chem-12-11-b09":{"idea":"Esterification joins an acid and an alcohol and loses water.","steps":["R–COOH + R'–OH ⇌ R–COOR' + H₂O.","A little strong acid is the catalyst.","The products are an ester and water.","The reaction is reversible, so an equilibrium forms."],"others":"An ether comes from two alcohols or a Williamson reaction. Only carbon dioxide would not be a product of esterification. A ketone with hydrogen gas is not the result of an acid and alcohol reaction.","answer":"An ester and water"},"chem-12-11-b10":{"idea":"A strong dehydrating acid removes water from an alcohol.","steps":["Concentrated H₂SO₄ with heat removes –OH from one carbon.","It removes H from the next carbon.","Together these leave as H₂O.","A C=C bond forms, so the product is an alkene."],"others":"An alkyne would need two molecules of water to go, which does not happen. An ether forms only at a lower temperature with excess alcohol. Concentrated H₂SO₄ here is not meant to oxidise to an acid.","trap":"High temperature gives alkene. Lower temperature, excess alcohol gives ether.","answer":"An alkene, by loss of H2O across two adjacent carbons"},"chem-12-11-b11":{"idea":"Count the –OH groups to name the type.","steps":["Glycerol is HOCH₂–CHOH–CH₂OH.","It has one –OH on each of the three carbons.","Three –OH groups make a trihydric alcohol."],"others":"A monohydric alcohol has one –OH and a dihydric one has two. Glycerol is not a phenol, because no –OH sits on an aromatic ring.","answer":"Trihydric alcohol"},"chem-12-11-b12":{"idea":"Phenol is a benzene ring with one H replaced by –OH.","steps":["Benzene is C₆H₆.","Replace one H by –OH: C₆H₅OH.","Count the atoms: C₆, H₆, O.","The formula is C₆H₆O."],"others":"C₆H₆O₂ is catechol, with two –OH groups. C₆H₁₂O is cyclohexanol, a saturated ring. C₇H₈O is benzyl alcohol, with one extra carbon.","answer":"C6H6O (that is, C6H5OH)"},"chem-12-11-b13":{"idea":"Simple ethers are named as an alkoxy group on the longer alkane.","steps":["CH₃–O–CH₂CH₃ has a methyl group and an ethyl group on oxygen.","The longer chain is the parent: ethane.","The shorter group with oxygen is methoxy.","The name is methoxyethane."],"others":"Propan-1-ol is an isomer but an alcohol, not an ether. Methyl ethanoate is an ester. Ethoxymethane takes the wrong group as the parent; the larger group should be the parent chain.","trap":"Smaller group becomes the alkoxy prefix.","answer":"Methoxyethane (an alkoxyalkane name)"},"chem-12-11-b14":{"idea":"Mass percent of an element is its mass in one mole divided by the molar mass.","steps":["Molar mass of CH₃OH = 32 g/mol.","Oxygen in it = 16 g.","% O = 16 / 32 × 100 = 50.","Check: C and H are (12 + 4) / 32 = 50 percent too."],"answer":"50 %"},"chem-12-11-b15":{"idea":"Same method: one mole of phenol has one oxygen atom.","steps":["Molar mass of C₆H₅OH = 94 g/mol.","Oxygen in it = 16 g.","% O = 16 / 94 × 100 = 17.02.","Check: C and H are 78 / 94 = 82.98 percent, and 100 − 82.98 = 17.02."],"answer":"17.02 %"},"chem-12-11-b16":{"idea":"The Lucas test shows how stable the carbocation is.","steps":["Lucas reagent is conc. HCl with anhydrous ZnCl₂.","The alcohol reacts by SN1 through a carbocation.","A tertiary carbocation is the most stable, so it forms fastest.","The insoluble alkyl chloride makes the mixture cloudy at once."],"others":"Primary alcohols form the least stable carbocation, so they are slow. Secondary alcohols go in about five minutes. The three do not react at the same rate.","trap":"Fast cloudiness at room temperature means tertiary.","answer":"Tertiary alcohols (via a stable carbocation, SN1)"},"chem-12-11-b17":{"idea":"In the Lucas test the slowest alcohols are primary.","steps":["A primary carbocation is very unstable.","So there is no SN1 reaction at room temperature.","Primary alcohols give no cloudiness unless heated.","Tertiary and secondary alcohols do react without heating."],"others":"Tertiary alcohols react at once. Secondary alcohols turn cloudy after some minutes. It is not true that none react even on heating; primary alcohols do react when heated.","trap":"Primary: no cloudiness at room temperature.","answer":"Primary alcohols"},"chem-12-11-b18":{"idea":"A mild oxidant stops at the aldehyde stage.","steps":["A primary alcohol R–CH₂–OH has two H on the carbinol carbon.","Removing one H from C and the H from O gives R–CHO.","PCC is mild and used without water.","So the oxidation stops at the aldehyde."],"others":"A ketone comes from a secondary alcohol, which has only one H on the carbinol carbon. A carboxylic acid needs a strong oxidant such as KMnO₄. An alkene comes from dehydration, not oxidation.","trap":"Mild oxidant gives aldehyde. Strong oxidant gives acid.","answer":"An aldehyde; under PCC the oxidation stops at that stage"},"chem-12-11-b19":{"idea":"A secondary alcohol has one H on the carbinol carbon.","steps":["The oxidant removes this H and the O–H hydrogen.","A C=O bond forms with two carbon groups on it.","That is a ketone.","A ketone has no H on the carbonyl carbon, so it does not oxidise further."],"others":"An aldehyde would come from a primary alcohol. A carboxylic acid needs a primary alcohol and a strong oxidant. It is not true that no reaction occurs; secondary alcohols oxidise easily.","trap":"Secondary gives ketone.","answer":"A ketone"},"chem-12-11-b20":{"idea":"Oxidation of an alcohol needs a C–H bond on the carbinol carbon.","steps":["The oxidant removes the O–H hydrogen and one C–H hydrogen.","A tertiary alcohol has three carbons on the carbinol carbon.","So it has no H on that carbon.","The usual oxidation cannot happen."],"others":"The number of carbons is not a rule on its own; the missing hydrogen is the reason. Tertiary alcohols are often soluble in such solutions. They certainly do contain an –OH group.","trap":"No C–H on the carbinol carbon: no easy oxidation.","answer":"The carbinol carbon has no hydrogen atom attached, so there is no C-H bond available to remove alongside the O-H"},"chem-12-11-b21":{"idea":"Phenol is more acidic because its conjugate base is stabilised by resonance.","steps":["Losing H⁺ from phenol gives the phenoxide ion.","The negative charge on O spreads into the ring by resonance.","The ethoxide ion cannot spread its charge this way.","A more stable base means a stronger acid."],"others":"The C–O bond in phenol is sp², not sp³, and it is shorter. The +I effect of ethyl does make ethoxide less stable, but this is not the main reason for phenol's strength. Phenol does not hydrogen bond to its own ring.","answer":"Phenoxide is resonance-stabilised: its negative charge spreads into the ring; ethoxide's cannot"},"chem-12-11-b22":{"idea":"Acid strength follows the stability of the conjugate base.","steps":["Carboxylate: charge shared equally by two oxygens, so it is the most stable.","Phenoxide: charge spread into the ring.","Water gives OH⁻, which has no spreading.","Ethoxide is the least stable, as the ethyl group pushes electrons."],"others":"The four are not equally acidic. The ethanol-first order is the reverse of the true order. Putting phenol above the carboxylic acid ignores the better resonance in carboxylate.","trap":"Acid > phenol > water > alcohol.","answer":"Carboxylic acid > phenol > water > ethanol"},"chem-12-11-b23":{"idea":"The –OH group pushes electrons into the ring.","steps":["The lone pair on O enters the ring by resonance (+M effect).","The extra charge sits on the ortho and para carbons.","These positions attract electrophiles.","So –OH is activating and o,p-directing."],"others":"Meta is the position for deactivating groups. Phenol reacts very easily, so not at all is wrong. The carbon with –OH (ipso) is not where the substitution mainly goes.","answer":"Ortho and para (since -OH is a strongly activating, o,p-directing group)"},"chem-12-11-b24":{"idea":"The Kolbe reaction puts a –COOH group on the ring.","steps":["Sodium phenoxide reacts with CO₂ under heat and pressure.","CO₂ is the electrophile and attacks ortho to O⁻.","Acid treatment then gives –COOH and –OH.","The product is salicylic acid."],"others":"Picric acid comes from nitration with a lot of nitric acid. Benzoic acid would lose the –OH. Salicylaldehyde is the Reimer–Tiemann product, not the Kolbe product.","trap":"Kolbe: CO₂, gives acid. Reimer–Tiemann: CHCl₃, gives aldehyde.","answer":"Salicylic acid (2-hydroxybenzoic acid)"},"chem-12-11-b25":{"idea":"The Reimer–Tiemann reaction puts a –CHO group on the ring.","steps":["CHCl₃ with NaOH makes dichlorocarbene, :CCl₂.","It attacks the phenoxide ring ortho to O⁻.","Hydrolysis turns the –CHCl₂ group into –CHO.","The product is salicylaldehyde."],"others":"Chlorobenzene would need Cl to replace –OH, which does not happen. Salicylic acid is made by the Kolbe reaction. The para acid is not formed from this reaction.","answer":"Salicylaldehyde (2-hydroxybenzaldehyde)"},"chem-12-11-b26":{"idea":"The Williamson synthesis joins an alkoxide and an alkyl halide by SN2.","steps":["C₂H₅O⁻ attacks the carbon of CH₃Br from the back.","Br⁻ leaves.","C₂H₅O⁻Na⁺ + CH₃Br → C₂H₅–O–CH₃ + NaBr.","The ether has one ethyl and one methyl group."],"others":"Diethyl ether would need ethyl bromide. An organic product does form, namely the ether. Ethanol and methanol would come from water, which is not present.","answer":"Methyl ethyl ether, CH3-O-C2H5"},"chem-12-11-b27":{"idea":"An unsymmetrical ether can be made by two routes, swapping the groups.","steps":["Target: CH₃–O–C₂H₅.","Route 1: C₂H₅O⁻ + CH₃Br.","Route 2: CH₃O⁻ + C₂H₅Br.","Both put one methyl and one ethyl on oxygen."],"others":"CH₃O⁻ with CH₃Br gives dimethyl ether. C₃H₇O⁻ with CH₃Br gives methyl propyl ether. C₂H₅O⁻ with C₂H₅Br gives diethyl ether.","trap":"Count the groups on each side of oxygen.","answer":"Sodium methoxide (CH3O-Na+) + bromoethane (C2H5Br)"},"chem-12-11-b28":{"idea":"Alkoxides are strong bases as well as nucleophiles.","steps":["A tertiary halide is crowded around the carbon.","SN2 back attack is hindered.","The alkoxide instead removes a β-hydrogen.","This E2 elimination gives an alkene, so the ether yield is poor."],"others":"Tertiary halides do react; they are not inert. Primary and tertiary halides give very different yields. Alkoxides do react with alkyl halides, and that is the Williamson reaction.","trap":"Use a primary halide with the bulky alkoxide, not the reverse.","answer":"The strongly basic alkoxide favours E2 elimination over SN2 substitution with a bulky, hindered tertiary substrate"},"chem-12-11-b29":{"idea":"Excess HI cleaves both C–O bonds of an ether.","steps":["R–O–R + HI → R–OH + R–I.","With excess HI and heat, R–OH + HI → R–I + H₂O.","So the final products are two R–I plus water."],"others":"The original alcohol is only the middle product, not the end. A carboxylic acid is not formed by HI. Ethers are not inert to HI.","trap":"Excess HI: two alkyl iodides.","answer":"Two molecules of alkyl iodide, R-I (plus water)"},"chem-12-11-b30":{"idea":"The Grignard carbon adds to the carbonyl carbon.","steps":["Formaldehyde, HCHO, has two H on the carbonyl carbon.","R adds to this carbon, giving R–CH₂–O–MgX.","Acid hydrolysis gives R–CH₂–OH.","The carbon has one carbon neighbour, so it is primary."],"others":"A secondary alcohol comes from other aldehydes. A tertiary alcohol comes from ketones. A carboxylic acid comes from CO₂, not formaldehyde.","trap":"HCHO is the only carbonyl that gives a primary alcohol.","answer":"A primary alcohol, R-CH2-OH"},"chem-12-11-b31":{"idea":"After the Grignard attack, count the carbon groups on the alcohol carbon.","steps":["The aldehyde R'–CHO has one H and one R' on the carbonyl carbon.","R adds, so the carbon holds H, R' and R.","After hydrolysis it is R–CH(OH)–R'.","Two carbon neighbours: a secondary alcohol."],"others":"A primary alcohol would need formaldehyde. A tertiary alcohol would need a ketone. An ether does not form in this reaction.","answer":"A secondary alcohol, R-CH(OH)-R'"},"chem-12-11-b32":{"idea":"A ketone has two carbon groups on the carbonyl carbon already.","steps":["The ketone R'–CO–R'' has R' and R''.","R from the Grignard reagent adds as a third carbon group.","After hydrolysis the carbon has –OH and three carbon groups.","This is a tertiary alcohol."],"others":"A primary alcohol needs formaldehyde. A secondary alcohol needs an aldehyde. Ketones do react with Grignard reagents.","answer":"A tertiary alcohol, R-C(OH)(R')(R'')"},"chem-12-11-b33":{"idea":"Use the mole ratio of 1 to 1 to find the ester mass.","steps":["Moles of acetic acid = 60 / 60 = 1 mol.","The ratio is 1 : 1, so 1 mol of ester forms.","Mass = 1 × 88 = 88 g.","Check: mass is conserved, 60 + 46 = 88 + 18 = 106 g."],"answer":"88 g"},"chem-12-11-b34":{"idea":"The carbon share of the molar mass gives the percentage.","steps":["Molar mass of C₆H₅OH = 94 g/mol.","Carbon in it = 6 × 12 = 72 g.","% C = 72 / 94 × 100 = 76.60.","Check: H and O are 22 / 94 = 23.40 percent, and 100 − 23.40 = 76.60."],"answer":"76.60 %"},"chem-12-11-b35":{"idea":"Degrees of unsaturation count rings and double bonds.","steps":["DoU = (2C + 2 − H) / 2.","For C₆H₆O: C = 6, H = 6.","DoU = (12 + 2 − 6) / 2 = 4.","Check: one ring plus three C=C bonds gives 4."],"answer":"4"},"chem-12-11-b36":{"idea":"Hydrogen bonding explains the large gap in boiling point between isomers.","steps":["Ethanol has an O–H group.","Its molecules hydrogen-bond to each other.","Dimethyl ether has no O–H, so it cannot do this.","Much less energy is needed to boil it, so its bp is about −24 °C."],"others":"Ether is less polar than ethanol in effect, and being more polar would not make it boil lower. Both have the same molar mass, so mass is not the cause. Ether cannot hydrogen-bond to itself at all.","trap":"Same formula, different bonding, very different bp.","answer":"Ethanol molecules H-bond to each other through O-H; dimethyl ether has no O-H"},"chem-12-11-b37":{"idea":"Anisole has an oxygen lone pair that can enter the ring, like phenol.","steps":["The O in –OCH₃ is bonded to the ring.","Its lone pair is donated into the ring (+M effect).","This activates the ortho and para positions.","The effect is a little weaker than with –OH."],"others":"The +I effect of methyl is small compared with the +M of oxygen. Steric bulk does not block the meta positions. –I does not make a group o,p-directing; here the group is +M.","answer":"-OCH3 donates its oxygen lone pair into the ring (+M), activating ortho/para, a bit less than -OH"},"chem-12-11-b38":{"idea":"Phenol's ring is so reactive that bromination needs no catalyst.","steps":["–OH strongly activates the ortho and para positions.","Br₂ in water attacks all three: two ortho and one para.","The product is 2,4,6-tribromophenol, a white precipitate.","Benzene needs FeBr₃ because its ring is much less reactive."],"others":"Bromobenzene would need –OH to be replaced, which does not happen. Bromine water does not carry FeBr₃. Mono-bromo products form only under mild conditions, not with excess bromine water.","trap":"White ppt with bromine water tests for phenol.","answer":"2,4,6-tribromophenol; -OH activates the ring so strongly that Br2 attacks without a catalyst"},"chem-12-11-b39":{"idea":"Find the –OH carbon and count its carbon neighbours.","steps":["Propan-2-ol is CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₃.","The carbon with –OH is carbon 2.","It is joined to two CH₃ groups.","Two carbon neighbours make it a secondary alcohol."],"others":"Primary would need one carbon neighbour. Tertiary would need three. Every alcohol has a class, so no defined class is wrong.","answer":"Secondary (the carbinol carbon is attached to 2 other carbons)"},"chem-12-11-b40":{"idea":"Ether oxygen has lone pairs, so it can take a proton.","steps":["The oxygen lone pair bonds to H⁺ from a strong acid.","This forms R₂O⁺–H, an oxonium ion.","The oxygen now has a positive charge.","It is the first step before cleavage."],"others":"An alkene or a carboxylic acid would need more steps. Ethers do react with strong acids, so no interaction is wrong.","answer":"An oxonium salt (the ether oxygen's lone pair bonds to H+, giving a positively charged oxygen)"},"chem-12-11-b41":{"idea":"Work out the product of each route and compare.","steps":["CH₃MgBr + propanal gives CH₃–CH(OH)–C₂H₅, which is 2-butanol.","C₂H₅MgBr + acetaldehyde gives the same compound.","But-1-ene with H₂O/H⁺ puts –OH on carbon 2, giving 2-butanol.","CH₃MgBr + butanal gives a 5-carbon alcohol, pentan-2-ol."],"others":"The propanal route gives 2-butanol. The acetaldehyde route gives 2-butanol. The hydration of but-1-ene follows Markovnikov's rule and also gives 2-butanol.","trap":"Add up the carbons: 2-butanol has four.","answer":"CH3MgBr + butanal (CH3CH2CH2CHO), then acidic hydrolysis"},"chem-12-11-b42":{"idea":"Both reactions use phenoxide, but the electrophiles differ.","steps":["Kolbe: CO₂ is the electrophile, so the ring gets –COOH.","The product is salicylic acid.","Reimer–Tiemann: :CCl₂ is the electrophile.","Hydrolysis gives –CHO, so the product is salicylaldehyde."],"others":"Kolbe keeps the –OH, so benzoic acid is wrong, and Reimer–Tiemann does not give chlorophenol. Only Kolbe uses CO₂. Swapping the two electrophiles reverses the truth.","answer":"Kolbe: CO2 is the electrophile, giving salicylic acid; Reimer-Tiemann: :CCl2 gives salicylaldehyde"},"chem-12-11-b43":{"idea":"Cleavage happens where substitution is possible.","steps":["In anisole, one C–O bond is to sp³ methyl and the other to sp² phenyl.","Iodide attacks the methyl carbon by SN2.","The sp² ring carbon cannot be attacked this way.","So the products are phenol and CH₃I."],"others":"An aryl cation is very unstable, so SN1 on the ring does not happen. The iodine and oxygen electronegativity argument does not decide the site. The aryl C–O bond is stronger, not weaker, because of resonance.","trap":"The ring carbon resists attack.","answer":"I- attacks the sp3 methyl carbon by SN2; the sp2 aryl carbon resists attack, so Ar-O stays intact"},"chem-12-11-b44":{"idea":"Find the limiting reagent first.","steps":["Moles of acetic acid = 30 / 60 = 0.5 mol.","Moles of ethanol = 30 / 46 = 0.652 mol, in excess.","Acetic acid limits, so 0.5 mol of ester forms.","Mass = 0.5 × 88 = 44.0 g."],"trap":"The smaller number of moles decides the yield.","answer":"44.0 g"},"chem-12-11-b45":{"idea":"What matters is the atom joined to the ring and the way electrons flow.","steps":["–OH has an oxygen with a lone pair attached to the ring.","It donates electrons into the ring (+M), so it activates ortho and para.","In –COOH, the ring carbon is attached to C=O, which pulls electrons out (–M).","So –COOH deactivates and directs meta."],"others":"Size does not decide the pattern. –OH is mainly electron-donating by resonance; calling it –I with o,p direction is not the real reason, and –COOH is not +I. Two oxygens in –COOH do not donate twice as much.","answer":"-OH donates its O lone pair into the ring (+M, o/p); -COOH's C=O pulls ring electrons out (-M, meta)"},"chem-12-11-b46":{"idea":"Alkyl groups and chlorine act in opposite ways.","steps":["Cl pulls electrons (–I), which stabilises the conjugate base.","Alkyl groups push electrons (+I).","This destabilises the alkoxide ion.","So tertiary alcohols are less acidic than primary ones."],"others":"Alkyl groups do not withdraw electrons. Chlorine acts by induction, not resonance. Tertiary alkoxides do form; their lower acidity is real and mainly due to +I and poorer solvation.","trap":"Compare the direction of the inductive effect first.","answer":"Alkyl groups donate electrons (+I) and destabilise the alkoxide ion, so tertiary alcohols are LESS acidic; Cl withdraws (-I), the opposite effect"},"chem-12-11-b47":{"idea":"The ring type decides the acidity.","steps":["Phenol has an aromatic ring: sp² carbons with 6 delocalised π electrons.","Its conjugate base spreads the charge into the ring.","Cyclohexanol has a saturated ring with no π system.","So it acts as an ordinary alcohol, weakly acidic."],"others":"The rings are not both saturated, and acidity does not depend on how many hydrogens the ring has. The aromatic ring is not strained, and the –OH in phenol is on an sp² carbon.","answer":"Phenol's ring is aromatic (sp2, 6 delocalised pi electrons); cyclohexanol's is saturated (sp3, no pi system)"},"chem-12-11-b48":{"idea":"Use the 1 to 1 mole ratio of the Williamson reaction.","steps":["Moles of C₂H₅ONa = 13.6 / 68 = 0.2 mol.","1 mol of alkoxide gives 1 mol of ether.","Mass of ether = 0.2 × 60 = 12.0 g.","Check: reactants 13.6 + 19.0 = 32.6 g, products 12.0 + 20.6 = 32.6 g."],"answer":"12.0 g"},"chem-12-11-b49":{"idea":"A negative charge makes a ring much better at attacking an electrophile.","steps":[":CCl₂ is electron-poor, so it is an electrophile.","Phenoxide has a full negative charge on oxygen.","The charge spreads into the ring by resonance.","The ring becomes very electron-rich and attacks :CCl₂."],"others":"Phenoxide is more electron-rich, not less. The –OH does not block the ortho positions. :CCl₂ is an electrophile, not a nucleophile.","trap":"That is why NaOH is needed.","answer":"Phenoxide's negative charge feeds into the ring by resonance, making it far more electron-rich toward :CCl2"},"chem-12-11-b50":{"idea":"The aldehyde needs water to oxidise further.","steps":["In water, an aldehyde adds H₂O to form a gem-diol, R–CH(OH)₂.","The gem-diol loses another H and becomes the acid.","PCC is used in dry solvents like dichloromethane.","With no water, the hydrate cannot form, so it stops at the aldehyde."],"others":"PCC does not turn acids back into aldehydes. It is weaker as an oxidant for a reason other than oxygen count. Aldehydes are oxidised easily by many oxidants.","answer":"PCC works without water, so the aldehyde cannot form the gem-diol (hydrate) needed to oxidise further"}};
